Q1. How do you comment code in Python? What are the different types of comments?
Comments in Python are identified with a hash symbol, #, and extend to the end of the line. Hash characters in a string are not considered comments, however. There are three ways to write a comment - as a separate line, beside the corresponding statement of code, or as a multi-line comment block.
Python programming gets complicated, including comment lines in Python is a great way to improve code readability. It is always recommended to include documentation and notes in Python syntax to make the code more readable and understandable for other coders. This is a useful practice when more than one programmer works on the same project.
Types of Comments in Python
There are three major types of comments in Python, and each of them has a different syntax. Let’s learn about each of them in the following section.

Single line Comments
You must start a single-line comment in Python with a hashtag symbol (#) with no white spaces. These comments end at the end of the line.
The syntax of a single-line comment is 
# comments here
If the comment exceeds a single line, place a hashtag # at the beginning of the next line and continue the comment.  
Example - 
#this is a single line comment
print(“hello”)    
#this function will print hello 


Multiline Comments
Unlike other programming languages that support multiline comments, such as Java and C, Python doesn’t have a specific feature to support multiline comments. However, that doesn’t imply that making multiline comments is completely impossible in Python. There are multiple ways to write multiline comments. Although these methods are not technically multiline comments.
Example-
#this is first comment
#this is second comment
The next way is to use string literals in Python but without assigning any variables. If we don’t assign a string literal to a variable, the interpreter ignores it. We can use it to write multiline comments and use either a single quote (‘’) or double quotes (“”).
Example-
'this is a string using as a comment'
Docstring Comments
Docstring, which is short for documentation string, is an in-built feature in Python used for associating documentation with functions, modules, objects, methods, and classes. It is a string literal with triple quotes and appears immediately after the function.
Docstrings are written in the first line after defining a function, module, method, etc., using triple quotations.
Example-
""" This is a sample of docstring but
we can also use it as a multilne comment"""

Q2. What are variables in Python? How do you declare and assign values to variables?
A variable in Python is a name that refers to a value stored in memory. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program.
Python allows you to declare a variable by simply assigning a value to a name using the = (assignment operator). There is no need to specify the data type, as Python is dynamically typed.
x = 10        
# Integer variable
name = "Alice"  
# String variable
price = 99.99   
# Float variable
is_valid = True 
# Boolean variable
Rules for Naming Variables in Python:
Variable names must start with a letter (A-Z or a-z) or an underscore _.

The rest of the name can contain letters, digits (0-9), or underscores.

Variable names are case-sensitive (age and Age are different variables).

Reserved keywords (like def, class, if, etc.) cannot be used as variable names.

Valid Variable Names:
my_var = 5
_name = "Ajay"
age1 = 25
PI = 3.14159
Invalid Variable Names (will cause an error):
1st_number = 10   # Cannot start with a number
my-variable = 20  # Cannot contain hyphens
class = "Python"  # Cannot use reserved keywords
Multiple Variable Assignments:
a, b, c = 1, 2, 3   # Assigns 1 to a, 2 to b, and 3 to c
x = y = z = 100     # Assigns 100 to x, y, and z

Creating Variables- 
Python has no command for declaring a variable.

A variable is created the moment you first assign a value to it.
x = 5
y = "Ajay"
print(x)
print(y)

5
